## Airlock pairs coding-agent policies with an audit collector

Source: Matt / Lunar Engineering · Show HN — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50419615
Published: 2026-11-09
Channel: hn

Airlock is an early framework for managing autonomous coding agents through policies kept in version control. Its creator describes the project in a first-party Show HN announcement, including an OpenTelemetry collector that stores selected agent actions for audits. The intended problem is managing agent permissions and accountability across a business rather than configuring each developer’s local assistant.

First, an agent run proves its identity using a GitHub-issued identity token. Airlock exchanges that proof for a short-lived GitHub token restricted to the repositories and permissions in the agent’s role file. Teams can then set a repository’s autonomy level and apply stricter overrides to individual files. A shadow mode lets them observe how the framework would behave before enabling it.

The audit collector records a subset of actions, so the announcement does not establish complete execution coverage. Prompt storage that only customers can decrypt remains exploratory. The creator says the schemas and reusable GitHub actions are public, while opening the backplane’s source is planned after further development.

Why it matters: Teams piloting fleet policies should test which agent actions reach the audit record and which remain outside its coverage.

